---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s6">
  <div class="num">06</div>
  <h2>Optional: review a change in Delta</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Your website is already working. You do not need Delta to keep it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Delta keeps a task's conversation and file changes together in a thread. I
    like that for reviewing work, but its <a
      href="https://delta.dev/docs/privacy-and-security/agentic-safety"
      >safety documentation</a
    >
    currently says agents have unrestricted device access and no permission prompts.
    A request to wait is only an instruction. Skip this exercise if you want enforced
    approvals.
  </p>
  <p>
    Adding a project uploads its files to Delta's servers, and later edits sync
    there too. Sync means keeping copies up to date. Use only the practice
    website and material you are comfortable uploading. Do not open your photo
    library, client work or an unfamiliar project. A backup helps recover files;
    it cannot undo a disclosure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Delta needs its own model access: a model is the AI doing the reasoning.
    Claude subscription sign-in is still marked "coming soon" in the
    <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/agents/models-and-providers"
      >provider docs</a
    >. A paid Zed plan with hosted AI, supplied through Zed's service, is one
    option without API keys. Check its usage charges before paying. You do not
    need to install the Zed editor, an app for changing text files. A Claude
    Code subscription alone is not enough.
  </p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Install and sign in'}>
      <p>
        Get Delta from <a href="https://delta.dev/download"
          >delta.dev/download</a
        >
        and follow its <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/installation"
          >installation instructions</a
        >. Macs need macOS 13 or later and Apple Silicon, not Intel. Windows
        users run the downloaded installer. Mac users unzip the app and move it
        to Applications. The documented Mac terminal step removes a system
        download warning, so read it before running it and use only the official
        download.
      </p>
      <p>
        Follow the <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/account/authenticate"
          >sign-in steps</a
        >
        to create a Zed account and accept the early access agreement. Sign-in uses
        GitHub, an online service for storing and sharing code; create an account
        there if needed. Connect model access during setup. Skip "Add your team" and
        complete "Welcome to Delta", including opening its terminal.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Prepare the project for Delta'}>
      <p>
        Delta opens a Git repository: a project folder with a saved history. Git
        is the program that keeps that history; a commit is one saved snapshot.
        Reopen Claude Code in <code class="i">my-site</code> using
        <a href="#s3">the manual-permissions command</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ask it to check whether Git is installed, then propose setting up Git
        and saving the website in an initial commit. Do not upload anything or
        change other folders. If Git is missing, use its
        <a href="https://git-scm.com/install/"
          >official installation instructions</a
        >. Approve the local setup after reviewing its plan, then close Claude
        Code.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Open the website folder'}>
      <p>
        In Delta, click Add Project and select <code class="i">my-site</code>,
        not its photos folder. Review the "Projects Sync to the Cloud" notice
        before continuing. In the project menu, choose Work In, then Existing
        Local Checkout. A checkout means the files you are working on. This
        choice edits your website directly, not an isolated copy. Keep your
        backup and run only one agent at a time.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is a setup gap: <a
      href="https://delta.dev/docs/concepts/delta-and-git">Delta &amp; Git</a
    >
    says a repository without a remote works locally, while
    <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/concepts/worktrees">Delta Worktrees</a> says a
    remote is required. A remote is another location Git can exchange history with,
    often online. If Delta refuses this local project, stop and message me. Do not
    publish your files to get past it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'First Delta request: only after setup succeeds'}
    code={'Read CLAUDE.md. In index.html, add the words "Maker and photographer" below my name. Use style.css for any styling. Change nothing else. Do not initiate any uploads or publishing beyond Delta\'s automatic syncing, and do not run Git commands. Show me a short plan first and wait for my ok before changing files. Afterward, list the files changed and explain how you checked the result.'}
    copy
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="4" title={'Run and review the change'}>
      <p>
        Send the request and read its plan before replying. When the change is
        ready, open Changes using the upper-right diff icon. A diff is a view of
        what was added and removed. Select each changed file. Select a line and
        start typing to comment, or send a normal message if that fails.
      </p>
      <p>
        Open your original <code class="i">index.html</code> in the browser and refresh
        it. Check the new words and the narrow layout. Ask for a correction if needed.
        There is no separate acceptance step in this workflow: the files have already
        changed. Close Delta before restoring files from your backup if you do not
        want the result.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <p>
    For help, send me the error text with private details removed. Sharing a
    thread is a separate access decision and may require an online Git copy;
    copying its link does not necessarily let me open it.
  </p>
</div>
